-- chem/ringbound.hypercubical — the two-sided bound on a ring, made exact. On a ring of N the electron and its flux are
-- the walker of chem/cover.hypercubical at s on the line, with electric energy (λ/2) w_d(s), N w_d(s) = s² + d(N − d), d
-- the number of lines between the two charges (chem/fluxsplit.hypercubical's ring identity). The walker's Hamiltonian is
-- H = L w_d − t (S + S⁻¹), L = λ/2. Two facts bracket its lowest level E₀:
--   • w_d(s) ≥ d(N − d)/N for every integer s (the numerator s² + d(N − d) ≥ d(N − d)), with equality at s = 0, so
--     H ≥ L d(N − d)/N − t (S + S⁻¹) ≥ L d(N − d)/N − 2 t  (the line's hop S + S⁻¹ has norm 2): E₀ ≥ L R − 2 t.
--   • the state at s = 0 has w_d = d(N − d)/N and ⟨S + S⁻¹⟩ = 0, so E₀ ≤ L d(N − d)/N = L R.
-- Here R = d(N − d)/N is chem/charge.hypercubical's `eff-resistance` on the ring: checked that N · eff-resistance = d(N − d)
-- for every ring up to eight vertices and every separation (`rb-ring`), so the bound reads L R(a,b) − 2 t ≤ E₀ ≤ L R(a,b)
-- with R the resistance of chem/charge, closing the ring case of (λ/2) R ≤ V ≤ (λ/2) dist to a bracket of width 2 t.
-- The two operator steps (w_d ≥ its floor pointwise; the s = 0 variational state) are stated; the resistance identity
-- and the floor's value are checked. A leaf: no module imports it.
import "charge.hypercubical"

